        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Previous studies have shown that astronauts are impaired while performing aiming and tracking tasks in space but scientists weren't sure why. The prevalent theory was that the vestibular system, which is known to be affected by microgravity, sends signals to other parts of the brain that control fine motor performance and cause performance deficits in the week or so it takes the brain to adapt to microgravity. In some early space experiments a loss of hand-eye coordination that could last for two to three weeks was observed in crewmembers. These losses were attributed to the absence of gravity interfering with normal brain activity. 
<p>
However, later experiments turned up little evidence in support of this so-called microgravity hypothesis. To test this hypothesis directly, the Perceptual Motor Deficits in Space (PMDIS) experiment was designed to investigate why astronauts experience difficulty with hand-eye coordination while on orbit. These measurements were used to distinguish between three possible explanations: the brain not adapting to the near weightlessness of space; the difficulty of performing fine movements when floating in space; and stress due to factors such as space sickness and sleep deprivation.
<p>
This experiment tested the theory that the loss of eye-hand coordination during space flight is due to the disruption of certain neural circuits in the human brain, arising from a disruption in the vestibular system. Understanding how the brain adapts to physiological changes that the International Space Station (ISS) crewmembers undergo are applicable on Earth as well as space. The results from this experiment gave insight on how the brain overcomes stresses that are not normally part of the day-to-day life. This new information can be applied in many areas of research that deal with neurological diseases in order to provide improved treatments. This experiment was a cooperative effort with the Canadian Space Agency.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Training on the experiment procedure was conducted over a four month period. Crew participants practiced performing the experiment prior to actual data gathering to establish stable baseline performance.  Data collection sessions were conducted approximately 30 days prior to launch; 71-94 days after launch; during flight; and approximately 30 days after landing. Reaction times and accuracy were assessed as the astronauts performed a series of hand/eye coordination exercises using the joystick, touchscreen, and hand reaction switch box. Complex multitasking tests were also performed and reaction times were assessed. PMDIS monitored the hand-eye coordination of astronauts in microgravity. Measurements were performed under seven conditions: 
<p>
• Tapping targets on a computer screen with a stylus (stylus, restrained and stylus, unrestrained)<br>
• Moving a cursor between the targets with a joystick (joystick, restrained and joystick, unrestrained) <br>
• Performing these tasks while responding to tones with a button press (Dual stylus or joystick restrained) <br>
• Responding to tones with a button press (simple response time)
<p>
During the PMDIS sessions, the crew was seated on the middeck floor of the Space Shuttle, securely attached with a waist belt. Using a joystick connected to a laptop computer, the astronaut moved a display cursor between two targets. In other trials, the astronaut tapped targets on a touchscreen using a stylus. The astronaut also listened to musical tones and pressed a button in response while hitting the targets to simulate multi-tasking. Subjects were not allowed to exercise within 20 minutes of starting the testing sessions.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
The results from PMDIS contradict the microgravity hypothesis because a major prediction of this hypothesis, that performance would be degraded using either the stylus or the joystick, was not supported. Rather, performance degradation only occurred with the more mentally demanding dual task using the joystick. These results suggested that the loss of hand-eye coordination in space can be attributed to a variety of interacting stressors which lead to cognitive overload. These factors include lack of body stability, degree of learning on the task, and its complexity, space sickness, and sleep deprivation.
        </div>

